WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:05.361
Throughout the debut season of "1883," Isabel
May's character Elsa Dutton serves as one

00:00:05.361 --> 00:00:07.400
of the show's biggest cornerstones.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:11.630
Not only does the young Dutton ancestor quickly
establish herself as a key protagonist in

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:15.400
the "Yellowstone" prequel spin-off, but she
also provides the narration for the Taylor

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.280
Sheridan-created show.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.100
One of the most intriguing qualities about
Elsa is her hardiness and her independence

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:25.650
— traits handed down from her parents, James
and Margaret Dutton, played by Tim McGraw

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:27.180
and Faith Hill.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:31.580
Throughout Season 1 of "1883," Elsa often
finds herself in some of the show's toughest

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:37.110
situations as she, her family, and other settlers
made their way from Texas to Oregon in hopes

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:38.170
of a new life.

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:42.120
Through every potentially disastrous or fatal
encounter, the Duttons pulled through as a

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:47.170
family unit, with Elsa providing audiences
a unique window in their mental and emotional

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:48.170
headspace.

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:52.780
Looking back, it's easy to pick out several
different instances and moments from "1883"

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:56.899
where fans were genuinely upset or affected
by what Elsa was going through.

00:00:56.899 --> 00:01:01.310
However, there's one scene in particular that
firmly stands out above the rest, with some

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:04.100
viewers calling it "almost unbearable" to
watch.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.600
The scene in question comes in the final moments
of the show's season finale.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.639
Elsa, is preparing to say her final goodbyes
as her condition has become increasingly worse.

00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:17.849
What happens next is a moment that "1883"
and "Yellowstone" fans will likely never forget.

00:01:17.849 --> 00:01:22.140
A majority of the finale episode is spent
trying to get her the medical care Elsa desperately

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.280
needs, even though it's clear the wound is
too severe; she's not long for this world.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:30.479
A heartbreaking decision is made: James Dutton
will travel with Elsa and wherever the pair

00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:34.530
stop to rest is the place where the Dutton
patriarch will lay down roots for his family's

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:35.719
new homestead.

00:01:35.719 --> 00:01:39.969
During Elsa's final moments, James takes his
daughter to an open area and they rest by

00:01:39.969 --> 00:01:44.229
a very specific tree — a tree that "Yellowstone"
fans quickly recognized.

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:45.229
She tells her father,

00:01:45.229 --> 00:01:46.229
"This is the spot.

00:01:46.229 --> 00:01:47.619
Let's just sit here.

00:01:47.619 --> 00:01:48.619
Rest for a bit."

00:01:48.619 --> 00:01:52.329
It's a poignant moment and one that's also
extremely heartbreaking, to the point where

00:01:52.329 --> 00:01:56.460
viewers were genuinely upset and angry with
Paramount for airing it.

00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:00.079
Elsa's death helps set up the backstory for
how the Dutton family ranch came to settle

00:02:00.079 --> 00:02:04.529
in Paradise Valley, Montana, the location
where the present-day Dutton family lives,

00:02:04.529 --> 00:02:06.109
works, and schemes.

00:02:06.109 --> 00:02:10.269
The untimely passing of James' eldest child
is unexpected and perhaps not the kind of

00:02:10.269 --> 00:02:12.769
finale plot twist fans were hoping to see.

00:02:12.769 --> 00:02:17.409
It feels like a cruel misdirect, having Elsa
taking on such an important position as a

00:02:17.409 --> 00:02:21.709
narrator and becoming a central figure, only
to be wiped off the board by something as

00:02:21.709 --> 00:02:24.120
treatable as an arrow wound (even by "1883"
standards).

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:29.629
And yet, there is something moving in knowing
that it is Elsa, a character audiences spend

00:02:29.629 --> 00:02:33.879
so much time with in Season 1, who will be
the metaphorical foundation on which Dutton

00:02:33.879 --> 00:02:36.060
Yellowstone Ranch is built.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:40.459
Although it's clear to see how Elsa's death
actually served to move the plot of "1883,"

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:44.690
as well as the saga of the Dutton family,
forward, fans were not pleased to see the

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:49.349
beloved character (or actress Isabel May)
exit the show, especially in such a tragic

00:02:49.349 --> 00:02:50.349
manner.

00:02:50.349 --> 00:02:51.349
One user tweeted,

00:02:51.349 --> 00:02:53.469
"Since Elsa died, I canceled."

00:02:53.469 --> 00:02:57.299
Another expressed, quote, "I can't live without
her," posting a petition online to request

00:02:57.299 --> 00:03:00.459
that Paramount Network bring May's character
back somehow.

00:03:00.459 --> 00:03:03.719
Their disgust flowed over into a second tweet,
which included this observation:

00:03:03.719 --> 00:03:07.849
"Elsa had to die because it explains how they
end up in Montana?

00:03:07.849 --> 00:03:09.170
How stupid!"

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:12.950
Elsewhere on Twitter, one fan explained, quote
"It's just too heartbreaking," and another

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:14.980
said simply, "So many tears."

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:17.749
Over on YouTube, viewers were just as upset.

00:03:17.749 --> 00:03:18.890
One user commented,

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:22.359
"That final scene was heartbreaking and almost
unbearable."

00:03:22.359 --> 00:03:23.780
Another YouTuber remarked,

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:25.650
"Won't be the same without [Elsa].

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:27.849
I can now cancel Paramount+."

00:03:27.849 --> 00:03:32.099
The death of Elsa was a moment that not only
weighed heavy on viewers but the actors as

00:03:32.099 --> 00:03:33.099
well.

00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:35.069
"1883" star Tim McGraw told Variety,

00:03:35.069 --> 00:03:38.849
"We literally couldn't read [the final scripts]
to each other because [Faith Hill and I] were

00:03:38.849 --> 00:03:39.849
crying so much.

00:03:39.849 --> 00:03:41.510
I mean, ugly, boo-hoo crying.

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:43.510
I was a blubbering idiot."

